<!--
  Author: Kris Hollingsworth
  Class: Math417/517-Real Analysis
  Date: 2026-08-07
  Updated:
  File: 03-cantors_second_diagonal.oml
  Description:
    See below from DoenetML Desc.
  Raw Link: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DoctorHaitch/doenet-math-problems/main/M417_517-real-analysis/03-cantors_second_diagonal.oml

  ## DoenetML Description
  Author: Kris Hollingsworth
  Date: 2026-08-07
  Purpose: |
    Small finite example to demonstrate the proof for |S|<|P(S)|
    proceeds. Students can build and rebuild sets from
    S={1,2,3,4} -> P(S) and see the set
    V = {x | x \notin f(x)} to see how regardless of their
    selections, V will never be in the image of f.

  Follow-up: |
    Have students explain why V can never be in Im(f). The logic
    is not dependent on finiteness, so this completes the proof
    for both finite and transfinite sets. Often trying to break
    things is an excellent way to understand them.

  Other explorations:
    - Create random functions and have students find the set V.
    - Enumerate the number of functions f for each possible set V.
      - Construct two different functions with the same diagonal set V.
        Determine how many functions correspond to a given V.
    - Classify functions by the size of V. Which cardinalities
      of V occur most frequently?
    - Find all functions for which V = ∅ and all functions for
      which V = S.
    - Compare injective, surjective, and arbitrary functions
      from S to P(S). How does the structure of V change?
    - Explain why this proof is equivalent to the example of
      Cantor's second diagonal argument for directly proving the existence
      of uncountable sets.
    - Examine how the proof changes when S has
      0, 1, 2, 3, or 4 elements.
      Look for patterns in the number of possible functions.
    - Compare the diagonal set V with the image Im(f).
      Experiment with ways to measure how "far" V is from appearing in the image.
    - Connect the example to Russell's paradox by comparing
      the condition x ∉ f(x) with the condition x ∉ x.
    - Investigate why the proof depends only on membership
      and logical negation, not on the size of S.
    - Explore transfinite cardinalities:
        ℵ₀ < 2^ℵ₀ < 2^(2^ℵ₀) < ...
    - Research the continuum hypothesis and identify where
      Cantor's theorem guarantees strict inequality but does
      not determine whether intermediate cardinalities exist.
    - Consider the inverse question:
      Can every set be realized as a power set of some other set?
      Is it true for some subsets of finite/transfinite sets?
      Compare cardinality constraints with structural constraints.
-->

<p>
  Use the interactive below to map elements of <m>S=\{1,2,3,4\}</m>
  to elements of <m>\mathcal P(S)</m> to create a function
  <m>f:S\to\mathcal P(S)</m>.
  
  The set <m>V=\left\{x~|~x\notin f(x) \right\}</m> is shown
  once you have defined a function. Try to make a function
  <m>f</m> so that <m>V\in \operatorname{Im}(f)</m>,
  the image of <m>f</m>.
</p>

<me>
  V=
  <conditionalContent
    condition="not($v[1].hide or $v[2].hide or $v[3].hide or $v[4].hide)"
  >
    \{<asList>
      <conditionalContent condition="$include_one">1</conditionalContent>
      <conditionalContent condition="$include_two">2</conditionalContent>
      <conditionalContent condition="$include_three">3</conditionalContent>
      <conditionalContent condition="$include_four">4</conditionalContent>
    </asList>\}
  </conditionalContent>
</me>

<setup>
  <!-- Move out of setup for debugging, state tracking variables. -->
  <group name="tail_setup">
    <numberList name="tail_state_list">0 0 0 0</numberList>
    <p>tail_index: <number name="tail_index" /></p>
    <number name="tail_state">
      $tail_state_list[1]+$tail_state_list[2]+$tail_state_list[3]+$tail_state_list[4]
    </number>
    <module name="trigger_tail_logic">
      <moduleAttributes>
        <number name="current_index" />
      </moduleAttributes>
      <p>Current_index: $current_index</p>
      <triggerSet triggerWhenObjectsClicked="$tails[$current_index]">
        <conditionalContent>
          <case
            condition="$tail_state_list[$current_index]=0 and $tail_state=0"
          >
            <updateValue
              target="$tail_index"
              type="number"
              newValue="$current_index"
            />
            <updateValue
              target="$tail_state_list[$current_index]"
              type="number"
              newValue="1"
            />
          </case>
          <case condition="$tail_state>0">
            <updateValue
              target="$tail_state_list[$current_index]"
              type="number"
              newValue="1"
            />
            <updateValue
              target="$tail_state_list[$tail_index]"
              type="number"
              newValue="0"
            />
            <updateValue
              target="$tail_index"
              type="number"
              newValue="$current_index"
            />
          </case>
        </conditionalContent>
      </triggerSet>
    </module>
    <module copy="$trigger_tail_logic" current_index="1" />
    <module copy="$trigger_tail_logic" current_index="2" />
    <module copy="$trigger_tail_logic" current_index="3" />
    <module copy="$trigger_tail_logic" current_index="4" />
  </group>
  <group name="head_setup">
    <module name="trigger_head_logic">
      <moduleAttributes>
        <number name="target_index" />
      </moduleAttributes>
      <p>target_index: $target_index</p>
      <triggerSet triggerWhenObjectsClicked="$heads[$target_index]">
        <conditionalContent condition="$tail_state=1">
          <updateValue
            target="$v[$tail_index].fixLocation"
            type="boolean"
            newValue="false"
          />
          <updateValue
            target="$v[$tail_index].head"
            type="math"
            newValue="$heads[$target_index]"
          />
          <updateValue
            target="$v[$tail_index].hide"
            type="boolean"
            newValue="false"
          />
          <updateValue
            target="$v[$tail_index].fixLocation"
            type="boolean"
            newValue="true"
          />
          <updateValue
            target="$tail_state_list[$tail_index]"
            type="number"
            newValue="0"
          />
        </conditionalContent>
      </triggerSet>
    </module>
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="1" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="2" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="3" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="4" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="5" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="6" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="7" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="8" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="9" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="10" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="11" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="12" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="13" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="14" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="15" />
    <module copy="$trigger_head_logic" target_index="16" />
  </group>
  <group>
    <boolean name="include_one">
      $v[1].head=$heads[1] or $v[1].head=$heads[3] or $v[1].head=$heads[4] or
      $v[1].head=$heads[5] or $v[1].head=$heads[9] or $v[1].head=$heads[10] or
      $v[1].head=$heads[11] or $v[1].head=$heads[15]
    </boolean>
    <boolean name="include_two">
      $v[2].head=$heads[1] or $v[2].head=$heads[2] or $v[2].head=$heads[4] or
      $v[2].head=$heads[5] or $v[2].head=$heads[7] or $v[2].head=$heads[8] or
      $v[2].head=$heads[11] or $v[2].head=$heads[14]
    </boolean>
    <boolean name="include_three">
      $v[3].head=$heads[1] or $v[3].head=$heads[2] or $v[3].head=$heads[3] or
      $v[3].head=$heads[5] or $v[3].head=$heads[6] or $v[3].head=$heads[8] or
      $v[3].head=$heads[10] or $v[3].head=$heads[13]
    </boolean>
    <boolean name="include_four">
      $v[4].head=$heads[1] or $v[4].head=$heads[2] or $v[4].head=$heads[3] or
      $v[4].head=$heads[4] or $v[4].head=$heads[5] or $v[4].head=$heads[6] or
      $v[4].head=$heads[8] or $v[4].head=$heads[12]
    </boolean>
  </group>
</setup>

<graph
  xMin="-1"
  xMax="6"
  yMin="0"
  yMax="6"
  showNavigation="false"
  displayXAxis="false"
  displayYAxis="false"
  name="cantorGraph"
  fixAxes
>
  <shortDescription>
    Diagram showing the relationship between the elements 1, 2, 3, and 4 and the
    subsets of {1,2,3,4}. The left side contains the four individual elements.
    The right side contains all sixteen subsets, including the empty set and the
    full set. Connections indicate which subsets contain a selected element.
  </shortDescription>
  <group name="tails" fixLocation>
    <point labelPosition="left" styleNumber="$tail_state_list[1]+1">
      (0,5)<label>1</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="left" styleNumber="$tail_state_list[2]+1">
      (0,3.66)<label>2</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="left" styleNumber="$tail_state_list[3]+1">
      (0,2.33)<label>3</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="left" styleNumber="$tail_state_list[4]+1">
      (0,1)<label>4</label>
    </point>
  </group> <number name="delta" hide>1/3</number>
  <number name="anchor" hide>5.5</number> <group name="heads" fixLocation>
    <point labelPosition="right">(4,$anchor)<label>{}</label></point>
    <point labelPosition="right">(4,$anchor-$delta)<label>{1}</label></point>
    <point labelPosition="right">(4,$anchor-2*$delta)<label>{2}</label></point>
    <point labelPosition="right">(4,$anchor-3*$delta)<label>{3}</label></point>
    <point labelPosition="right">(4,$anchor-4*$delta)<label>{4}</label></point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-5*$delta)<label>{1,2}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-6*$delta)<label>{1,3}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-7*$delta)<label>{1,4}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-8*$delta)<label>{2,3}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-9*$delta)<label>{2,4}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-10*$delta)<label>{3,4}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-11*$delta)<label>{1,2,3}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-12*$delta)<label>{1,2,4}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-13*$delta)<label>{1,3,4}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-14*$delta)<label>{2,3,4}</label>
    </point>
    <point labelPosition="right">
      (4,$anchor-15*$delta)<label>{1,2,3,4}</label>
    </point>
  </group> <group name="v">
    <vector name="v1" styleNumber="3" tail="$tails[1]" hide />
    <vector name="v1" styleNumber="3" tail="$tails[2]" hide />
    <vector name="v1" styleNumber="3" tail="$tails[3]" hide />
    <vector name="v1" styleNumber="3" tail="$tails[4]" hide />
  </group>
</graph>